// The `prelude.oauth` sub-module: on-demand OAuth bearer tokens. `token` routes to the runtime's
// `oauth` reactor (in-runtime, like `http.fetch`) and so performs `io`. The runtime holds the token
// material and refreshes it; no token ever enters the program, and a credential that needs a human
// pauses the run on an authorization escalation rather than failing it.

@"The OAuth token could not be resolved for a TRANSIENT reason — a network error, or the token endpoint
failing (5xx) while refreshing. Thrown by `token`; a retried call re-resolves. (A credential that needs
a human to (re)authorize is never a throw: the run pauses on an authorization escalation instead.)"
data server_error(message: string)

@"Resolve a usable OAuth bearer token for the stored credential @name@, as a private string — it may
flow only to a submission sink such as an http `Authorization` header, never to a user-facing boundary.
When @name@ has no usable token (never authorized, or refresh-dead) the run pauses on a
`prelude.oauth.authorize` escalation and resumes once the credential is established; a transient
resolution failure throws `server_error`.

Ask on every use rather than holding the answer in a `var`: the runtime serves the stored token while
its recorded expiry holds (with a ~60s refresh margin) and refreshes once due, so the runtime owns the
caching. That recorded expiry is the only input, so a token revoked before it expires keeps being
served and the API answers 401 / 403 for up to one access-token lifetime; converting that downstream
failure into a `supervise.interrupted` recovers, since each re-run asks afresh."
external agent token(
  @"The registered name of a stored credential (e.g. \"github\"); the name itself is not a secret." name: string,
) -> string of private with io | prelude.throw[server_error] from "oauth"
